  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nIt will continue to be a pleasant afternoon and evening across \nthe region with temperatures into the lower 80s and low humidity\nprevailing. Diurnally driven cu will dissipate with the loss of\nheating this evening and this combined with light winds and low\ndew points will allow for another night of radiational cooling.\nThere are some suggestions that a bit of high level cirrus may \narrive late tonight but this tends to not have a significant \nimpact on cooling. As a result, used mostly MOS guidance for \nforecast lows with most places falling into the upper 40s to \nlower 50s. Valley fog is likely once again overnight and there \ncould also be some fog towards the Mid Coast and adjacent \nislands. \n\nSurface high pressure will persist on Sunday into Sunday night,\nallowing for continued dry conditions with near average\ntemperatures. High temperatures will be into the 70s to lower\n80s (coolest along the coast), with low temperatures mostly \ninto the lower 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nGlobal models are in good agreement that an unsettled pattern \ndevelops next week as several shortwaves look to traverse to the\nbroader 500 mb trough that has been sitting over the region for\nquite some time now. However, this is about all that the models\nagree on at the moment as the extent of precipitation we may \nsee as a result is very much up in the air. The Euro ensemble \nhas maintained its position of having a seasonably strong \nvertically stacked low pressure move through the area in the \nTuesday-Wednesday timeframe. This is combined with stronger \nmoisture transport bringing PWATs up to 1.5 to 1.75 inches south\nof the mountains, and as a result the ECMWF Ensemble is the \nmost bullish on QPF with high probabilities (50-60%) of \nwidespread amounts greater than an inch, and some moderate \nprobabilities (30-40%) starting to show up for amounts greater \nthan 2 inches. The GEFS and Canadian Ensemble are currently not \nfavoring the development of an upper low, and even keep the \nsurface low more to our south. The GEFS also has much weaker \nmoisture transport with PWATs in the 1.25 to 1.5 range. As a \nresult the current probabilities for more than an inch of QPF is\nvery low (~10%). Unsurprisingly this is making the current NBM \nspread very large with the 25th percentile around a tenth of an \ninch and the 75th percentile upwards of 2.5 inches. To say there\nis currently uncertainty in the rainfall amounts is an \nunderstatement, but the hope is that over the next couple days \nthe RAOB network will begin sampling this developing pattern, \nand can better inform the models. \n\nThunderstorms are possible each afternoon as instability \ngathers, even with daytime temperatures bordering on the \nseasonably cool side. At this point there isn't anything that \nscreams severe weather, but the uncertainty in low pressure \ntracks isn't helping that case either. I think the best way to \nsummarize the long term at this point is that there is a high \nconfidence in seeing periods of showers (and some thunderstorms)\nwhich may be heavy at times, but exact amounts, the track of \nlow pressure, and the duration low pressure lingers are very low\nconfidence and trends are going to have to be monitored closely\nover the coming days.",
